{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices across most horizons; apply an upward level shift (≈ +1 to +3 €/t on average), with larger uplift in strong uptrends.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the market breaks higher (multi-day rally), the model follows too slowly; add a momentum term so the next 1–15 days are lifted more than the back end.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction is strongest in the short-to-mid horizon (roughly days 3–20); taper the correction toward the far horizon unless momentum remains positive.",
    "Missed volatility spikes: the model smooths peaks and troughs; widen forecast dispersion around recent volatility regimes (e.g., after large daily moves, increase amplitude rather than reverting immediately).",
    "Late-horizon path errors: the model sometimes projects a late drop that does not materialize (examples 1–2) or projects a late surge that fails (examples 3–4); dampen far-horizon extrapolation by pulling days 20–30 toward a trend-consistent mean (blend with recent 10–15d average + momentum).",
    "Asymmetry after rallies: after strong upside moves, the model’s mean-reversion is too aggressive; reduce mean-reversion strength when recent returns are positive and accelerating."
  ]
}
